VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L3 — Two-class method for participating securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Glendale Corp. has 500,000 common shares and 100,000 participating preferred shares that receive dividends dollar-for-dollar with common shares after the preferred base dividend of $2.00 per share is paid. Net income is $1,800,000 and total preferred dividends declared are $200,000. The controller computed basic EPS using only the traditional formula. How does the existence of participating preferred stock affect the EPS calculation?', '["Basic EPS must be computed using the two-class method, which allocates undistributed earnings between common and participating preferred shares", "Basic EPS is unaffected because preferred dividends are simply subtracted from the numerator", "Participating preferred shares are only considered in diluted EPS under the treasury stock method", "The participation feature is ignored unless the preferred shares are convertible"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, participating securities require the two-class method for basic EPS. Undistributed earnings are allocated between common and participating preferred shares based on their respective participation rights. Choice B is incorrect because simply deducting the preferred dividend ignores the participation feature, which entitles preferred holders to share in undistributed earnings beyond their base dividend.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Definition of complex capital structure
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which type of capital structure requires the presentation of both basic and diluted EPS on the income statement?', '["Simple capital structure", "Complex capital structure", "Hybrid capital structure", "Consolidated capital structure"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, a complex capital structure exists when an entity has outstanding securities that could potentially dilute basic EPS, such as stock options, convertible bonds, or warrants. Entities with complex capital structures must present both basic and diluted EPS. Choice A is incorrect because a simple capital structure has no potentially dilutive securities and only requires basic EPS disclosure.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Discontinued operations separate EPS line
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Meridian Industries reports income from continuing operations of $2,400,000 and a loss from discontinued operations (net of tax) of $600,000 for the year ended December 31, 2025. The company has 400,000 weighted-average common shares outstanding. The CFO presents only a single EPS figure of $4.50. What correction is required under ASC 260?', '["No correction is needed because a single EPS figure is acceptable", "EPS must be presented separately for continuing operations and discontinued operations", "EPS for discontinued operations should be combined with other comprehensive income", "Discontinued operations reduce diluted EPS but not basic EPS"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, EPS must be presented for income from continuing operations and for net income on the face of the income statement. When discontinued operations exist, a separate per-share amount must be presented either on the income statement or in the notes. Choice A is incorrect because a single combined EPS figure fails to provide users with the required disaggregated information about recurring versus nonrecurring earnings.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Written put options dilutive effect
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Prescott Holdings wrote put options on 20,000 of its own common shares at a strike price of $55 per share. The average market price during 2025 was $40 per share. The company has 300,000 weighted-average shares outstanding and net income of $1,200,000. How do these written put options affect diluted EPS?', '["The options are antidilutive and excluded from diluted EPS", "The options add incremental shares to the denominator, reducing diluted EPS", "The options reduce the numerator by the premium received", "The options are dilutive under the reverse treasury stock method, adding incremental shares to the denominator"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options are dilutive when the exercise price exceeds the average market price. The reverse treasury stock method assumes the entity uses proceeds from issuing shares at market price to buy back shares at the higher strike price, resulting in a net increase in the denominator. Choice A is incorrect because written puts are dilutive (not antidilutive) when the strike price exceeds market price — the opposite of the rule for call options.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: easy / L2 — Weighted-average shares with treasury stock repurchase
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Briarwood Inc. had 250,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On September 1, 2025, the company repurchased 30,000 shares as treasury stock. What is the weighted-average number of common shares for basic EPS?', '["240,000", "230,000", "250,000", "220,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares are weighted by the portion of the period they were outstanding. The 250,000 shares were outstanding for 8/12 of the year, and 220,000 shares for 4/12: (250,000 x 8/12) + (220,000 x 4/12) = 166,667 + 73,333 = 240,000. Choice C (250,000) is incorrect because it ignores the mid-year repurchase that reduced outstanding shares.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Mandatorily convertible instrument EPS treatment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hawthorne Corp. issued $1,000,000 of mandatorily convertible bonds on January 1, 2025, which will automatically convert into 50,000 common shares on December 31, 2027. The bonds carry a 5% coupon and the tax rate is 25%. Hawthorne has net income of $900,000 and 200,000 weighted-average common shares. What is diluted EPS?', '["$3.60", "$3.70", "$3.75", "$3.50"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, mandatorily convertible securities are included in diluted EPS using the if-converted method. The numerator adds back the after-tax interest: $1,000,000 x 5% x (1 - 0.25) = $37,500. Diluted EPS = ($900,000 + $37,500) / (200,000 + 50,000) = $937,500 / 250,000 = $3.75. Choice A ($3.60) is incorrect because it fails to add back the after-tax interest expense to the numerator.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Bonus element in rights offering
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Alderton Corp. has 600,000 common shares outstanding and issues rights to existing shareholders to purchase one new share for every four shares held at $15 per share. The market price before the rights offering is $25 per share. The theoretical ex-rights price is $23 per share. What adjustment factor must be applied to retroactively restate prior-period EPS?', '["1.087", "0.920", "1.000", "0.600"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-13, when a rights offering includes a bonus element (exercise price below market), prior-period EPS is restated using an adjustment factor equal to the market price before the offering divided by the theoretical ex-rights price: $25 / $23 = 1.087. The weighted-average shares of prior periods are multiplied by this factor. Choice B (0.920) is incorrect because it inverts the ratio — the factor must be greater than 1.0 to reflect the dilutive bonus element.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — ASC 260 scope: who must present EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which entities are required to present earnings per share on the face of the income statement?', '["All entities that prepare GAAP financial statements", "Only entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock", "Only SEC-registered entities", "Only entities with complex capital structures"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required only for entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock (such as options or convertible securities) on an open market. Private companies are not required to present EPS. Choice A is incorrect because many private entities prepare GAAP financial statements but are exempt from the EPS presentation requirement.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Effect of share repurchase on basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakridge Manufacturing has net income of $3,000,000 and 500,000 weighted-average shares outstanding, yielding basic EPS of $6.00. On November 1, the company announces a share buyback of 100,000 shares. Assuming the repurchase is completed on November 1, what is the most likely effect on current-year basic EPS?', '["Basic EPS increases to $6.20 because weighted-average shares decrease", "Basic EPS decreases because the buyback reduces net income", "Basic EPS remains $6.00 because buybacks are ignored for EPS purposes", "Basic EPS increases to $6.25 because the full 100,000 shares are removed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, repurchased shares reduce the weighted-average count from the repurchase date. Removing 100,000 shares for 2/12 of the year reduces weighted-average shares by approximately 16,667, to 483,333. Basic EPS = $3,000,000 / 483,333 = $6.21, approximately $6.20. Choice D ($6.25) is incorrect because it treats the full 100,000 shares as removed for the entire year rather than weighting them for only the 2 months remaining.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Evaluate whether to include convertible debt or options first in diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Summit Financial Corp. reports net income of $5,000,000 with 1,000,000 weighted-average shares. It has: (1) 25,000 stock options with exercise price $30 and average market price $50, producing 10,000 incremental shares; and (2) $2,000,000 of 8% convertible bonds converting into 80,000 shares (tax rate 25%). The controller included the convertible bonds first and then added the options, arriving at diluted EPS of $4.74. Evaluate whether the sequencing was correct and determine the proper diluted EPS.', '["The sequencing was correct; diluted EPS is $4.74", "The options should be tested first because they have no numerator impact; diluted EPS is $4.85", "The options should be tested first because they have no numerator impact; diluted EPS is $4.70", "Both securities are antidilutive and should be excluded"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, potentially dilutive securities must be ranked from most dilutive to least dilutive. Options have zero numerator impact and are always tested first. Options add 10,000 shares: $5,000,000 / 1,010,000 = $4.95. Then test bonds: per-share impact = ($160,000 x 0.75) / 80,000 = $1.50, which is less than $4.95, so bonds are dilutive. Including bonds: ($5,000,000 + $120,000) / (1,010,000 + 80,000) = $5,120,000 / 1,090,000 = $4.70. The correct sequencing yields $4.70, not $4.74. Choice A is incorrect because including bonds before options misstates the incremental dilution test.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Basic EPS numerator for preferred dividends
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What amount is subtracted from net income in the numerator of the basic EPS formula when cumulative preferred dividends exist?', '["Only preferred dividends actually declared during the period", "The full annual cumulative preferred dividend, whether declared or not", "Preferred dividends paid in cash during the period", "No subtraction is needed for cumulative preferred stock"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, for cumulative preferred stock, the full annual dividend is subtracted from the basic EPS numerator regardless of whether dividends were declared. This is because cumulative dividends accumulate as a claim against future earnings. Choice A is incorrect because it describes the treatment for noncumulative preferred stock, where only dividends actually declared are deducted.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Definition of diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What does diluted earnings per share represent under ASC 260?', '["Earnings per share assuming all potentially dilutive securities are exercised or converted", "Earnings per share after deducting all preferred dividends", "Earnings per share using only shares outstanding at period end", "Earnings per share excluding discontinued operations"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-16, diluted EPS reflects the maximum potential dilution from all securities that could convert into or create additional common shares, such as options, warrants, and convertible instruments. It provides a worst-case view of per-share earnings. Choice C is incorrect because diluted EPS uses weighted-average shares plus the dilutive effect of potential common shares, not simply shares outstanding at period end.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Treasury stock method definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which method is used to calculate the dilutive effect of stock options and warrants on diluted EPS?', '["If-converted method", "Treasury stock method", "Equity method", "Cost method"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method is used for stock options and warrants. This method assumes the options are exercised at the beginning of the period, proceeds are used to repurchase shares at the average market price, and only the incremental net shares are added to the denominator. Choice A (if-converted method) is incorrect because it applies to convertible bonds and convertible preferred stock, not to options and warrants.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Contingently issuable shares not yet meeting condition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgeline Corp. has an agreement to issue 40,000 additional shares to former owners of an acquired business if cumulative earnings exceed $5,000,000 over three years. At the end of Year 2, cumulative earnings total $3,100,000. How should Ridgeline treat these contingently issuable shares in its Year 2 EPS calculations?', '["Include all 40,000 shares in basic EPS because the contingency period has not ended", "Include the shares in diluted EPS only if the earnings condition would be met assuming current-period earnings continue", "Exclude the shares from both basic and diluted EPS until the contingency is actually satisfied", "Include the shares in both basic and diluted EPS because partial fulfillment has occurred"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in diluted EPS if the contingency conditions would be satisfied assuming the end of the reporting period were the end of the contingency period. Since Year 2 cumulative earnings ($3,100,000) do not yet meet the $5,000,000 target, the shares are excluded from basic EPS but may be included in diluted EPS only through this hypothetical test. Choice C is incorrect because ASC 260 requires testing contingent shares for diluted EPS even before final satisfaction.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — Evaluate antidilution when net loss exists with convertible preferred
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinecrest Corp. reports a net loss of $800,000 with 200,000 weighted-average common shares. The company has 5,000 shares of $100 par, 7% convertible preferred stock, each converting into 5 common shares. No preferred dividends were declared. The junior accountant computed diluted EPS by adding back the $35,000 preferred dividend and dividing by 225,000 shares, arriving at a diluted loss per share of $3.40. Evaluate whether this calculation is correct.', '["The calculation is correct because the if-converted method applies to all convertible preferred stock", "The calculation is incorrect; the preferred dividend should not be added back because it was not declared", "The calculation is incorrect; in a net loss period, all potentially dilutive securities are antidilutive and must be excluded from diluted EPS", "The calculation is incorrect; diluted EPS should equal $3.56 after adjusting only the denominator"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, when a company reports a net loss from continuing operations, all potentially dilutive securities are antidilutive because their inclusion would reduce the loss per share. Basic EPS loss = ($800,000 - $35,000 cumulative preferred) / 200,000 = ($4.18). Diluted EPS must equal basic EPS. Choice A is incorrect because even though the if-converted method normally applies, the antidilution prohibition overrides it in loss periods.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Compute EPS with mid-year stock dividend
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fairview Industries had 400,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On March 1, it declared a 20% stock dividend. On October 1, it issued 60,000 new shares for cash. Net income for 2025 was $2,475,000. What is basic EPS?', '["$5.00", "$5.28", "$4.89", "$5.50"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-13, stock dividends are treated as if they occurred at the beginning of the earliest period presented. January 1 shares become 400,000 x 1.20 = 480,000 for the full year. The October cash issuance adds 60,000 x 3/12 = 15,000 weighted shares (not adjusted for the prior stock dividend since issued after). Weighted-average = 480,000 + 15,000 = 495,000. Basic EPS = $2,475,000 / 495,000 = $5.00. Choice B ($5.28) is incorrect because it fails to retroactively adjust the January 1 shares for the stock dividend.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — If-converted method for convertible preferred stock
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Cedarpoint Inc. has net income of $1,500,000 and 300,000 weighted-average common shares. The company has 10,000 shares of $50 par, 8% convertible preferred stock, each converting into 3 common shares. Basic EPS is $4.87. What is diluted EPS?', '["$4.87", "$4.55", "$4.35", "$4.17"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method adds back preferred dividends to the numerator and adds converted shares to the denominator. Preferred dividends = 10,000 x $50 x 8% = $40,000. Basic EPS numerator = $1,500,000 - $40,000 = $1,460,000. Diluted numerator = $1,500,000 (add back $40,000). Diluted denominator = 300,000 + 30,000 = 330,000. Diluted EPS = $1,500,000 / 330,000 = $4.55. Choice D ($4.17) is incorrect because it incorrectly doubles the converted shares or misapplies the conversion ratio.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Where EPS is presented
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, where must earnings per share amounts be presented for public entities?', '["In the notes to the financial statements only", "On the face of the income statement", "In the statement of stockholders'' equity", "In management''s discussion and analysis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, entities with publicly traded common stock must present EPS on the face of the income statement for income from continuing operations and for net income. Presentation in the notes alone is insufficient. Choice A is incorrect because while supplemental EPS data may appear in notes, the primary EPS figures must be on the income statement face.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Antidilutive securities exclusion rule
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what is the required treatment of antidilutive securities in the diluted EPS calculation?', '["They are included in diluted EPS to show maximum dilution", "They are excluded from the diluted EPS calculation", "They reduce the numerator but not the denominator", "They are averaged with dilutive securities"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-17, antidilutive securities must be excluded from diluted EPS because their inclusion would increase EPS or decrease the loss per share, misleading users about potential dilution. However, antidilutive securities must be disclosed in the notes. Choice A is incorrect because including antidilutive securities would overstate earnings per share, which contradicts the conservative purpose of diluted EPS.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Computing incremental shares for options with treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Stratton Corp. has 15,000 stock options outstanding with an exercise price of $35. The average market price of Stratton''s common stock during 2025 was $70 per share. Using the treasury stock method, how many incremental shares are added to the denominator for diluted EPS?', '["15,000", "7,500", "10,000", "5,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-23, the treasury stock method assumes options are exercised, generating proceeds of 15,000 x $35 = $525,000. These proceeds hypothetically repurchase $525,000 / $70 = 7,500 shares. Incremental shares = 15,000 issued - 7,500 repurchased = 7,500. Choice A (15,000) is incorrect because it assumes all option shares are added without accounting for the shares repurchased with the exercise proceeds.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — EPS with convertible bonds issued mid-year
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On July 1, 2025, Thornhill Industries issued $5,000,000 of 6% convertible bonds at par, convertible into 100,000 common shares. The tax rate is 25%. Net income for 2025 is $4,200,000 and weighted-average shares are 800,000. What after-tax interest adjustment is added to the diluted EPS numerator for these bonds?', '["$225,000", "$150,000", "$112,500", "$300,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method adds back after-tax interest for the period the bonds were outstanding. Interest for 6 months = $5,000,000 x 6% x 6/12 = $150,000. After-tax adjustment = $150,000 x (1 - 0.25) = $112,500. The converted shares are also weighted for 6/12 = 50,000 shares. Choice B ($150,000) is incorrect because it uses the pre-tax interest amount rather than the after-tax amount required for the numerator adjustment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — Evaluate whether to report EPS for OCI
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Brighton Healthcare''s board requests that the annual report include EPS figures for other comprehensive income items, including unrealized gains on available-for-sale debt securities of $450,000. The CFO argues this presentation is prohibited. The controller counters that voluntary EPS disclosure for OCI enhances transparency. Evaluate which position is correct under ASC 260.', '["The CFO is correct; ASC 260 prohibits presenting EPS for any component of OCI", "The controller is correct; ASC 260 requires EPS disclosure for all OCI components", "The controller is correct; ASC 260 permits but does not require voluntary EPS disclosure for OCI components", "Neither is correct; EPS for OCI is required only for foreign currency translation adjustments"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3A, an entity may voluntarily present per-share amounts for individual components of OCI, but it is not required. If presented, the entity must use the same denominator (weighted-average shares) used for basic and diluted EPS and disclose the basis of computation. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 does not prohibit this disclosure — it simply does not mandate it, making voluntary presentation acceptable.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Weighted-average concept purpose
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Why does ASC 260 require the use of weighted-average common shares outstanding rather than end-of-period shares for the basic EPS denominator?', '["To reflect the time period over which shares were available to generate earnings", "To minimize reported EPS for conservative financial reporting", "To simplify calculations when stock dividends occur", "To comply with SEC fair disclosure requirements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, weighted-average shares reflect the proportion of the reporting period during which each share participated in generating earnings. This matching principle ensures the denominator aligns with the earnings period. Choice B is incorrect because the purpose of weighting is accuracy in matching shares to the period they were outstanding, not conservatism.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Compute basic EPS with mid-year preferred dividend adjustment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Westbrook Corp. reports net income of $1,850,000 for 2025. The company has 400,000 weighted-average common shares and 8,000 shares of $75 par, 6% cumulative preferred stock outstanding all year. What is basic EPS?', '["$4.63", "$4.54", "$4.72", "$4.36"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, cumulative preferred dividends are deducted from net income regardless of declaration. Preferred dividends = 8,000 x $75 x 6% = $36,000. Basic EPS numerator = $1,850,000 - $36,000 = $1,814,000. Basic EPS = $1,814,000 / 400,000 = $4.535, or $4.54. Choice C ($4.72) is incorrect because it fails to deduct the full cumulative preferred dividend from the numerator.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Multi-security diluted EPS ordering and antidilution cutoff
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hillcrest Corp. reports net income of $2,000,000 with 500,000 weighted-average shares (basic EPS = $4.00). Three potentially dilutive securities exist: (A) options adding 8,000 incremental shares, (B) 4% convertible bonds adding $24,000 after-tax interest and 12,000 shares (per-share impact $2.00), and (C) 9% convertible preferred adding $90,000 dividends and 15,000 shares (per-share impact $6.00). Determine the correct diluted EPS after proper sequencing.', '["$3.89", "$3.94", "$3.85", "$4.00"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, securities are ranked from most dilutive to least. Order: options ($0 impact), bonds ($2.00), preferred ($6.00). Step 1 — options: $2,000,000 / 508,000 = $3.94. Step 2 — bonds: ($2,000,000 + $24,000) / 520,000 = $3.89, still dilutive so include. Step 3 — preferred: $6.00 per-share impact exceeds $3.89, making it antidilutive, so exclude. Diluted EPS = $3.89. Choice D ($4.00) is incorrect because it excludes all dilutive securities and equals basic EPS.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — Effect of stock split on comparative EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Waverly Inc. reported basic EPS of $8.00 for 2024. On March 1, 2025, the company declared a 2-for-1 stock split. When presenting 2025 comparative financial statements, what is the restated 2024 basic EPS?', '["$8.00", "$4.00", "$16.00", "$6.00"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-13, stock splits are applied retroactively to all prior periods presented for comparability. The 2-for-1 split doubles the share count, so the 2024 basic EPS of $8.00 must be restated to $8.00 / 2 = $4.00. Choice A ($8.00) is incorrect because failing to retroactively adjust prior-period EPS would make the 2024 figure incomparable with 2025, misleading financial statement users.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Dilutive impact analysis when market price barely exceeds exercise price
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Redstone Corp. has 20,000 stock options with an exercise price of $48 per share. During 2025, the average market price was $50. The company has 500,000 weighted-average shares and net income of $2,500,000 (basic EPS = $5.00). What is the most likely effect of these options on diluted EPS?', '["The options significantly reduce diluted EPS to approximately $4.50", "The options are antidilutive and excluded from diluted EPS", "The options add only 800 incremental shares, producing a minimal reduction in diluted EPS", "The options add 20,000 incremental shares to the denominator"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-23, the treasury stock method yields incremental shares of 20,000 - (20,000 x $48 / $50) = 20,000 - 19,200 = 800 shares. When the exercise price is close to the market price, the dilutive impact is minimal. Diluted EPS = $2,500,000 / 500,800 = $4.99, virtually unchanged. Choice D is incorrect because the treasury stock method nets the exercised shares against repurchased shares — only the 800 incremental shares are added, not all 20,000.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Compute diluted EPS with both options and convertible bonds
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ashland Corp. has net income of $3,200,000, 800,000 weighted-average shares, 10,000 stock options (exercise price $25, average market price $50) producing 5,000 incremental shares, and $1,000,000 of 5% convertible bonds converting into 20,000 shares (tax rate 25%). What is diluted EPS?', '["$3.88", "$3.92", "$3.95", "$4.00"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, options are tested first (no numerator impact): $3,200,000 / 805,000 = $3.98. Then test bonds: add $1,000,000 x 5% x 0.75 = $37,500 to numerator and 20,000 shares. Diluted EPS = ($3,200,000 + $37,500) / (805,000 + 20,000) = $3,237,500 / 825,000 = $3.92. Choice D ($4.00) is incorrect because it is the basic EPS figure and ignores the dilutive impact of both options and convertible bonds.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: hard / L3 — Year-to-date EPS vs quarterly EPS reconciliation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Maplebrook Inc. earned $600,000 in Q1, $400,000 in Q2, $800,000 in Q3, and $700,000 in Q4 of 2025. Weighted-average shares were 200,000 for Q1-Q2 and 250,000 for Q3-Q4 (due to a July 1 issuance of 50,000 shares). An analyst sums the four quarterly EPS figures ($3.00 + $2.00 + $3.20 + $2.80 = $11.00) and expects this to equal annual EPS. What is the correct annual basic EPS and why does the sum of quarterly EPS differ?', '["$11.11; the difference arises because the weighted-average denominator changes across quarters, making quarterly EPS non-additive", "$11.00; the quarterly sum equals annual EPS when properly computed", "$10.00; the analyst should use the annual weighted-average of 250,000 shares for all quarters", "$10.50; each quarter must use the year-to-date weighted-average share count"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, annual EPS uses the annual weighted-average shares: (200,000 x 6/12) + (250,000 x 6/12) = 225,000. Annual basic EPS = $2,500,000 / 225,000 = $11.11. The sum of quarterly EPS ($11.00) differs because each quarter uses its own weighted-average denominator, and these varying denominators make quarterly EPS figures non-additive. Choice B is incorrect because the mathematical sum of quarterly EPS only equals annual EPS when the share count remains constant throughout the year.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Evaluate impact of share-based compensation on EPS complexity
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Northgate Systems grants 50,000 restricted stock units (RSUs) on January 1, 2025, that vest over three years. During 2025, 12,000 RSUs are forfeited and the average unrecognized compensation cost is $800,000 (average market price $40). The company has net income of $6,000,000 and 1,000,000 weighted-average shares. The intern argues that all 50,000 RSUs should be added to diluted EPS denominator. Evaluate this position and determine the correct treatment.', '["The intern is correct; all granted RSUs are included regardless of vesting", "Only the 38,000 non-forfeited RSUs are added in full to the denominator", "RSUs use the treasury stock method with unrecognized compensation as assumed proceeds, reducing incremental shares", "RSUs are excluded from diluted EPS until the vesting conditions are fully satisfied"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28A, unvested RSUs are included in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method. Assumed proceeds include unrecognized compensation cost ($800,000), which hypothetically repurchases $800,000 / $40 = 20,000 shares. Incremental shares = 38,000 - 20,000 = 18,000. Choice A is incorrect because forfeited RSUs are excluded, and the treasury stock method reduces the incremental count by shares assumed repurchased with unrecognized compensation cost.', 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
